---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import MergeTool from '../components/MergeTool.tsx';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'Merge Photo and Signature into One Image | SizeTune';
const description =
  'Put a photo and a signature on one picture, stacked or side by side, then shrink it to the KB limit an upload form asks for. Done in your browser.';
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune: merge photo and signature', '/merge-photo-and-signature/', description);
---
<Base pageType="tool" width="wide" title={title} description={description} path="/merge-photo-and-signature/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Hero title="Merge a photo and a signature" lead="Some forms want your photo and signature as a single image. Add both, choose stacked or side by side, set a size limit, and download one picture. Nothing is uploaded." />

  <MergeTool client:load />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-4">
      This tool works inside your browser, so it will not run while JavaScript is turned off. Enable JavaScript and
      reload to merge your pictures. The notes below explain how the layouts work.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div class={`mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-10 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>When you need one picture</h2>
    <p>
      Most upload forms take a photo and a signature as two separate files, and for those the <a href="/resize-image-to-50kb/">photo resizer</a> and the <a href="/resize-signature/">signature resizer</a> are
      the right tools. A few forms ask for both on a single image, often with one size limit. This page puts the two
      pictures together without overlapping them and then shrinks the result to your limit.
    </p>

    <h2>Stacked or side by side</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Photo above signature.</strong> Both pictures are scaled to the output width you choose, with the photo on top and the signature below. A wide signature keeps its shape and the whole image is taller than it is wide.</li>
      <li><strong>Side by side.</strong> Both pictures sit on one row at the same height, and together they fill the output width. This gives a wide, short image, but each picture gets less room, so a small signature can become hard to read.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Output width and size limit</h2>
    <p>
      The output width sets how many pixels wide the merged picture is. Wider means more detail and a larger file. The
      size limit is passed to the resizer on this page, which lowers the JPEG quality and then the pixel size until the
      file fits. If the limit is too small for the picture, the resizer tells you what it reached instead of pretending.
    </p>

    <h2>Tips</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Crop the signature to its ink first with the <a href="/resize-signature/">signature resizer</a> so it is not mostly white space.</li>
      <li>Check your form's rules: some want the signature in a fixed position or size, which this page does not set.</li>
      <li>A white background is added behind both pictures. Transparent parts of a PNG become white.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <h3>Does the merged picture lose quality?</h3>
    <p>
      The pictures are drawn onto a new canvas and saved as a JPEG, so some detail is lost, and more is lost the lower
      your size limit is. Keep the original files.
    </p>
    <h3>Can I change which one goes first?</h3>
    <p>
      The photo is always first, above or on the left. If your form needs the other order, merge them here and tell the
      form where each part is, or ask whether it accepts two files.
    </p>
    <h3>Are my pictures uploaded?</h3>
    <p>
      No. They are merged in your browser and gone when you close or reload the page. Read the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for details.
    </p>
  </div>
  <RelatedTools path="/merge-photo-and-signature/" />
</Base>
